{
  "description": "MAINTAINER RULING #984, the run-lifetime rule: `the run's lifetime is the main line's business -- an every handler does not extend it`. Once the main line has finished and any ALREADY-STARTED handler body has completed, the run closes and queued-but-unstarted occurrences are discarded. Here the handler's own body outruns its interval: registered at tick 0 with interval 3 and a body that takes 4 ticks, it starts at tick 3 and its next interval arrives at tick 6 while it is still running, so that occurrence is queued. The body completes at tick 7, the queued occurrence is drained immediately (second `print`), and THAT invocation's body in turn overruns and queues another. The run does not chase it: the main line's `wait 3` is spent, so the program terminates cleanly with exactly TWO prints and NO diagnostic. This is the fixture that separates `drain the queue` from `manufacture ticks the main line never asked for`: draining in a loop until the queues empty runs this program back to back until the instruction budget stops it, producing hundreds of thousands of prints and an `ol-limit`. A learner who wants the timer to keep firing says so explicitly -- see `every-overrunning-handler-runs-back-to-back-under-forever`, which is where back-to-back running IS the specified behaviour.",
